---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const gramCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Grammar is patterns, not regulations', note: 'Descriptive grammar (what speakers actually do) vs prescriptive (what style guides demand). Learn the patterns; ignore the Victorian baggage ("no split infinitives" — Star Trek\'s "to boldly go" was right).' },
    { math: '2 · Patterns before paradigms', note: 'Learn chunks ("I would have…", "if I were…", "there is/are") as wholes — the brain stores multi-word patterns natively. Dissect them later; fluency first, analysis second.' },
    { math: '3 · Input reveals the pattern\'s function', note: 'Grammar study sticks after you\'ve MET the pattern in input: notice it (input), then learn its name (study), then use it (output). The reverse order — rules first, practice later — produces the textbook learner who can parse but not speak.' },
    { math: '4 · Errors are data, not sins', note: 'Errors map exactly what your current system believes. Fossilised errors need targeted attention (recording yourself reveals them); transient ones fix themselves with input. The research-methods lesson\'s discipline, applied to your own speech.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/intermediate/grammar-without-tears">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Grammar is best learned as <strong>patterns noticed in real input, then sharpened by
    conscious study, then cemented by use</strong> — in that order. Textbook grammar-first
    learning produces people who can conjugate verbs and can't speak; grammar-free learning
    produces fluent speakers who plateau. The sequence integrates both.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="descriptive patterns, chunks, the notice–study–use loop, error data" widget="equationStep" config={gramCfg} />

  <h2>Practical grammar strategy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One pattern at a time, to mastery:</strong> spend a week noticing past tense everywhere (input), drill its chunks (study), then force yourself to use it in output (practice). Serial focus beats parallel confusion.</li>
    <li><strong>Translate the hard bits of YOUR life:</strong> the grammar you need is the grammar your own sentences require — keep a log of things you wanted to say and couldn't. That log is your personal curriculum.</li>
    <li><strong>Case systems and genders:</strong> learn them through高频 chunks rather than tables (the 1,000-words lesson's logic) — the table is the reference, the chunks are the acquisition.</li>
    <li><strong>The linguistics ladder helps:</strong> knowing what morphology and syntax ARE (the linguistics lessons) makes any language's grammar legible in hours instead of weeks — grammar vocabulary is a metalanguage you can reuse across languages.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the linguistics ladder's syntax/morphology lessons; Batstone, "Grammar"; Schmidt, "Noticing" (1990).</p>
</TopicPage>
